  "description": "Two MEASURED closed forms for the q-degree of hecke_core_symbolic.build_atom_nf_sym, neither derived. (1) The shortfall of the max-plus bound is gap = 6N - 19 + [Z > N] for Z >= 5 and N >= 5, with zero exceptions on the tested grid -- so once a nucleus carries five or more protons the leading-order loss depends on the NEUTRON COUNT ALONE, linearly, at 6 degrees per neutron, independent of Z. Z <= 4 is a larger regime but converges to the same slope: Z=2 and Z=4 both settle to +6 per neutron with different offsets, and Z=0 alone settles to +8, making the pure-neutron chain an outlier in slope rather than only in offset. (2) The A=27 break in the companion probe's triangular law is one clean two-branch maximum: deg(gamma) = A(A+1)/2 + 2*max(0, A-26), verified A=7..40, two branches with the SAME quadratic leading term whose linear parts differ by 2A-52 and cross exactly at A=26; deg(sigma0) = deg(gamma) - 1 holds for A=8..40, across the break too, so it is not a reordering of which component dominates; A=7 is the sole exception at offset +7 and is the lower edge of the triangular window itself. NOT DERIVED: no mechanism for the coefficient 6, the constant -19, the Z >= 5 threshold, or the crossover at 26. These are fits to exact integer data over a STATED range, and the range is stated because the companion probe's triangular law looked exact over twenty consecutive values before breaking at the twenty-first.",
